{
  "case_id": "2009-08-810",
  "document_hash": "11fff840523367424f4e6f21f08306e6626ffdc6d18a788c431ad4f2b8138b10",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2009-08-810",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "11fff840523367424f4e6f21f08306e6626ffdc6d18a788c431ad4f2b8138b10",
      "evidence_id": "ev-11fff8405233-0000-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 1,
        "start": 1
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "ROBERT A. LONG, JR., ESQ., Washington, D.C.; on behalf of Petitioners."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2009-08-810",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "11fff840523367424f4e6f21f08306e6626ffdc6d18a788c431ad4f2b8138b10",
      "evidence_id": "ev-11fff8405233-0000-0001",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 1,
        "start": 1
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MATTHEW D. ROBERTS, ESQ., Assistant to the Solicitor General, Department of Justice, Washington, D.C.; on behalf of the United States, as amicus curiae, supporting the Respondents."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Robert A. Long, Jr., on behalf of the Petitioners",
      "case_id": "2009-08-810",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "11fff840523367424f4e6f21f08306e6626ffdc6d18a788c431ad4f2b8138b10",
      "evidence_id": "ev-11fff8405233-0000-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "In this ERISA case, the court of appeals applied a deferential standard of review to the district court's interpretation of the Xerox plan, but not to the plan administrator's interpretation. We think the court of appeals got it backwards."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2009-08-810",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "11fff840523367424f4e6f21f08306e6626ffdc6d18a788c431ad4f2b8138b10",
      "evidence_id": "ev-11fff8405233-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: Right. But -- but when the administrator has interpreted the plan incorrectly and the court finds -- the court of appeals finds that he has interpreted it incorrectly, it doesn't have to send it back and say, you know: Give me another bid; try something else."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2009-08-810",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "11fff840523367424f4e6f21f08306e6626ffdc6d18a788c431ad4f2b8138b10",
      "evidence_id": "ev-11fff8405233-0000-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 9,
        "start": 9
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: -- referring to Scott, but -- that this case -- what I took away from the Second Circuit's opinion was the flaw here was not that the method was no good if you had adequate notice; the flaw was the people affected were not told in what is the language of ERISA, in plain, simple language, what their entitlement was."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief actuaries",
      "case_id": "2009-08-810",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "11fff840523367424f4e6f21f08306e6626ffdc6d18a788c431ad4f2b8138b10",
      "evidence_id": "ev-11fff8405233-0000-0006",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 10,
        "start": 10
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the chief actuaries have filed an amicus brief saying this is quite typical, so this --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Respondents",
      "case_id": "2009-08-810",
      "confidence": 0.93,
      "document_hash": "11fff840523367424f4e6f21f08306e6626ffdc6d18a788c431ad4f2b8138b10",
      "evidence_id": "ev-11fff8405233-0001-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 22,
        "start": 22
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "And if you accept this interpretation that the district court adopted, and then the court of appeals said: Well, we will just give it deferential review; we won't even give it de novo review, it's -- it's, you know, one reasonable interpretation among many --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Respondents",
      "case_id": "2009-08-810",
      "confidence": 0.92,
      "document_hash": "11fff840523367424f4e6f21f08306e6626ffdc6d18a788c431ad4f2b8138b10",
      "evidence_id": "ev-11fff8405233-0001-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 25,
        "start": 25
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Well, with -- with respect, Justice Alito, that is absolutely ridiculous. I mean, no employer would do that to their current employees. That would treat the current employees like suckers."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2009-08-810",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "11fff840523367424f4e6f21f08306e6626ffdc6d18a788c431ad4f2b8138b10",
      "evidence_id": "ev-11fff8405233-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 29,
        "start": 29
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "But even if the -- even if no deference was owed to the administrator, could you explain why the task for the district court was not then simply to interpret what the plan means?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Stris",
      "case_id": "2009-08-810",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "11fff840523367424f4e6f21f08306e6626ffdc6d18a788c431ad4f2b8138b10",
      "evidence_id": "ev-11fff8405233-0002-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 42,
        "start": 41
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I don't think so. This is a defined benefits plan, and -- you know, from a regulatory standpoint, as this case comes to this Court, it is a defined benefits plan. Section 9.6 of the plan talks about non-duplication."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2009-08-810",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "11fff840523367424f4e6f21f08306e6626ffdc6d18a788c431ad4f2b8138b10",
      "evidence_id": "ev-11fff8405233-0002-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 43,
        "start": 43
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: Excuse me. Are you talking about the court of appeals sending it back to the district court, or are you talking about the district court sending it back to the administrator?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2009-08-810",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "11fff840523367424f4e6f21f08306e6626ffdc6d18a788c431ad4f2b8138b10",
      "evidence_id": "ev-11fff8405233-0002-0003",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 43,
        "start": 43
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "when the court of appeals sent it back to the district court, the district court never even considered sending it to the administrator because there would be no reason to do that."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Matthew D. Roberts",
      "case_id": "2009-08-810",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "11fff840523367424f4e6f21f08306e6626ffdc6d18a788c431ad4f2b8138b10",
      "evidence_id": "ev-11fff8405233-0002-0001",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 51,
        "start": 50
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "When a plan administrator has abused its discretion in construing plan terms, courts are not required to defer to the plan administrator's fallback interpretation of the same terms. That rule follows from trust law, and a contrary rule would undermine ERISA's protections for plan participants."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2009-08-810",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "11fff840523367424f4e6f21f08306e6626ffdc6d18a788c431ad4f2b8138b10",
      "evidence_id": "ev-11fff8405233-0003-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 60,
        "start": 60
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: Is it -- is it -- Mr. Roberts, is it a remedy? So that\u2019s -- one thing is you can view this as the district court as substitute interpreter of the plan, or another way you can look at it is to say, the -- the benefit determination was wrong, we reject it, the court rejects it. So, now, there is a remedy for that wrongful determination. So is this, what\u2019s going on in the district court, an interpretation of the plan or a remedy for a wrongful determination?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioners",
      "case_id": "2009-08-810",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "11fff840523367424f4e6f21f08306e6626ffdc6d18a788c431ad4f2b8138b10",
      "evidence_id": "ev-11fff8405233-0003-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 61,
        "start": 61
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. LONG: The remaining plan terms are not silent. Section 9.6 says that the offset should be the accrued benefit attributable to the prior lump-sum distribution, and that's an annuity payable at age 65. So there is plan language."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2009-08-810",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "11fff840523367424f4e6f21f08306e6626ffdc6d18a788c431ad4f2b8138b10",
      "evidence_id": "ev-11fff8405233-0003-0003",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 62,
        "start": 62
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the Solicitor General, in its brief on the merits to this Court did -- retracted that suggestion that the plan was silent."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioners",
      "case_id": "2009-08-810",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "11fff840523367424f4e6f21f08306e6626ffdc6d18a788c431ad4f2b8138b10",
      "evidence_id": "ev-11fff8405233-0003-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 63,
        "start": 63
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "There\u2019s actually Second Circuit law, the McCarthy against Dun & Bradstreet case, that holds that a summary plan description does not have to completely explain how you do every offset and actuarial adjustment."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2009-08-810",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "11fff840523367424f4e6f21f08306e6626ffdc6d18a788c431ad4f2b8138b10",
      "evidence_id": "ev-11fff8405233-0003-0004",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 65,
        "start": 65
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The case is submitted."
    },
    {
